"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[32509],{532509:e=>{e.exports='# Diagnose a Message Failure\n\n**USE THIS SKILL** when the user asks to:\n\n- Diagnose, debug, or "fix" a specific Genie space message\n- "Why did Genie return this answer / wrong number / wrong column?"\n- "Genie is overcounting…", "Genie is using the wrong date column…", "Genie made up this definition…"\n- Free-text variants like "Help me diagnose this message failure" — when accompanied by `## Message Under Investigation` in your context, the author triggered this from the chat footer icon\n\nWhen this skill applies, **immediately start retrieving context**. Do NOT ask clarifying questions before reading the message. After step 2, decide whether the specific failure is clear; if not, ask one targeted question (see "Optional Clarifications" below).\n\n## Context\n\nThe user is a Genie space author who got an incorrect or unhelpful answer from Genie and wants you to find the root cause and propose concrete context fixes that will improve Genie\'s behavior on similar future questions. You have full read/write access to the space\'s tables, instructions, knowledge snippets, columns, and benchmarks.\n\nThe agent\'s context already includes `## Current Genie Space` (with the **ID**) and, when a Genie space conversation is open, `## Current Conversation` (with **Conversation ID**). Use those as the conversationId when fetching message details. Do not invent or guess IDs.\n\nThis skill is grounded in the [Databricks Genie troubleshooting guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/troubleshooting). The failure-mode taxonomy and fix recommendations align with that page.\n\n## Investigation Steps\n\n### Step 1 — Identify the target message\n\nThis skill has two entry paths and you need to handle both:\n\n**Path A — icon-triggered.** Your context contains `## Message Under Investigation` with a **Conversation ID** and **Message ID**. Use those directly. No search is needed; proceed to step 2.\n\n**Path B — free-text prompt.** The author typed a description of the failure (e.g. "Genie is overcounting total messages — fix this", "Why did Genie use opp_date instead of cls_date?", "Genie keeps refusing to answer questions about NPS"). The author is almost always asking about a message in the Genie space conversation they have open right now, so **default to searching only that conversation.** The conversationId is in your context under `## Current Conversation`.\n\nFor Path B, work through these in order — pick the first one that resolves:\n\n1. **The author pasted a message ID.** If their prompt contains a token that looks like a message ID (a UUID/long opaque string, not a quoted English fragment), use it as the messageId together with the conversationId from `## Current Conversation`. No search needed.\n2. **The author pasted a deep link.** Genie URLs encode both IDs:\n   - Chat: `/genie/rooms/<dataRoomId>/chat/<conversationId>?m=<messageId>`\n   - Monitoring: `/genie/rooms/<dataRoomId>/monitoring?mc=<conversationId>&m=<messageId>`\n\n   Extract both from the URL and use them. A pasted link **overrides** the open-conversation default, since it tells you exactly which conversation/message the author meant.\n\n3. **The author described the message in plain words.** Call `readCurrentConversation` to list the messages in the open conversation. Match against the author\'s description using:\n   - **Content keywords** from the prompt — column names, table names, business terms.\n   - **Numeric values** the author quoted.\n   - **Failure signals** — prefer `feedback_rating: THUMBS_DOWN` and `status: FAILED` over plain `COMPLETED`.\n   - **Recency** within the conversation when nothing else discriminates.\n\n   If a single message clearly wins, confirm with one short line ("Diagnosing your <date> message about <topic> — let me dig in.") and proceed. If 2–3 candidates are close, list them with date + a short snippet and ask the author to pick. If the author named a recurring pattern across many messages ("Genie always uses the wrong date column"), pick the most recent representative thumbs-down or `COMPLETED` example in this conversation, say so explicitly, and proceed.\n\n4. **Nothing in the open conversation matches.** Ask the author for more context: "I don\'t see a message in this conversation that matches that description. Could you paste a link to the message, the message ID, or copy a bit of the prompt or Genie\'s answer?" Do not silently widen the search to other conversations.\n5. **`## Current Conversation` is missing from your context.** The author is on a non-chat page (rare for this skill). Ask them to open the Genie space conversation containing the failed message and trigger the diagnose flow again, or paste a link to the message.\n\nOnce you\'ve established `conversationId` + `messageId`, pass both to `getMessageDetails` in step 2 — the tool accepts arbitrary IDs, so a pasted link to a different conversation works too.\n\n### Step 2 — Read the failing message in detail\n\nCall `getMessageDetails` with the `conversationId` and `messageId` you established in step 1 (icon-triggered: from `## Message Under Investigation`; free-text: from a pasted ID/link, or from the candidate you picked while reading the open conversation). The response is split into three sections — read **all three**:\n\n1. **User prompt + result** — what the user actually asked, the message status (`COMPLETED`, `FAILED`, etc.), feedback rating (thumbs-up/down), feedback categories, message-level error.\n2. **Context applied to this message** — the diagnostic core. This is what the LLM was given to produce its answer:\n   - `progress_report` smart-context retrievals: `relevant_tables`, `primitive_instructions` (text instructions), `knowledge_store_instructions` (snippets) — what Genie\'s smart-context step decided was relevant _before_ generating.\n   - Per query attachment: `relevant_instruction_text` (the **literal markdown text instructions** the LLM saw), `relevant_sql_instructions` (SQL example/snippet IDs cited + justification), `query_references`, `assumptions`, `thinking_process`.\n3. **Genie\'s response** — generated SQL, error, description, and any text response.\n\n### Step 3 — Decide whether the specific failure is clear\n\nAfter step 2, the specific failure is **clear** if any of these hold:\n\n- The message has `status: FAILED` with a concrete error.\n- The message has `feedback_rating: THUMBS_DOWN` together with `feedback_categories` (e.g. `WRONG_CALCULATIONS`, `MISSING_COLUMNS`) or a thumbs-down comment that names the issue.\n- The user\'s free-text prompt names the issue (e.g. "Genie is overcounting messages — should be COUNT DISTINCT").\n\nThe specific failure is **not clear** if:\n\n- `## Message Under Investigation` is set (icon-triggered) and the message looks plausible (`COMPLETED`, no thumbs-down, no error). The author saw something wrong but hasn\'t told you what.\n- The user said "this is wrong" or "fix this" without specifying what part of the answer is incorrect.\n\nIf the failure is not clear, **stop and ask** before diagnosing (see Optional Clarifications). Otherwise continue to step 4.\n\n### Step 4 — Read the surrounding conversation\n\nRetrieve the current conversation to see prior turns, follow-ups, and regenerations (the message\'s `related_message_id` from step 2 points at any prior version). This often reveals whether the user already corrected Genie, retried with rephrasing, or hit the same failure repeatedly.\n\n### Step 5 — Resolve cited instructions and read full space context\n\n- For each instruction ID cited in step 2 (from `primitive_instructions`, `knowledge_store_instructions`, `relevant_sql_instructions`, `query_references`), retrieve the full instruction list once and look up the bodies. This completes the picture of what was actually in the LLM\'s context.\n- Retrieve the space configuration (tables, description, run-as).\n- For each table referenced in the failing SQL, retrieve the per-table configuration (column descriptions, synonyms, visibility, entity-matching state, value indexing).\n- If the answer might depend on existing benchmarks, retrieve the curated questions of type `BENCHMARK`.\n\n### Step 6 — Diagnose the root cause\n\nClassify the failure into one of these modes (the taxonomy is _guidance, not a fixed rule_ — propose the most appropriate fix for the specific issue, even if it differs from the recommendations below):\n\n#### Incorrect SQL Logic\n\n- **Pattern:** Genie\'s SQL is mechanically wrong for the prompt. Example: user asks for "total messages sent" and Genie writes `COUNT(*)` instead of `COUNT(DISTINCT message_id)`.\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Add a SQL expression for the standardized measure definition.\n  - If the expression already exists but Genie isn\'t applying it, extend its synonyms/title so the user phrasing matches.\n  - Add an example SQL question/answer pair demonstrating correct usage.\n\n#### Inconsistency\n\n- **Pattern:** A business term has multiple competing definitions across instructions, snippets, or columns. Example: "churned users" is sometimes "stopped sending messages" and sometimes "stopped any action".\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Resolve conflicting instructions so a single canonical definition remains. Update the wording or remove duplicates.\n  - Hide overlapping columns that confuse the model.\n  - Add a SQL expression that captures the canonical definition.\n  - Add an example SQL pair for the standardized answer to that prompt.\n\n#### Misapplied Semantics\n\n- **Pattern:** Genie picks a plausible-but-wrong column, table, or filter. Example: user asks "closed deals this month", Genie filters on `opp_date` (open date) instead of `cls_date` (close date).\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Clarify column descriptions and synonyms so similar fields aren\'t confusable.\n  - Add a SQL expression for the standardized semantic; add synonyms to existing expressions if Genie isn\'t applying them accurately.\n  - Hide unnecessary columns that confuse Genie.\n  - Enable **example values** / **value dictionaries** on filter columns so Genie recognizes valid values; refresh values when new data is added.\n  - Define foreign-key references in Unity Catalog (or join relationships in the knowledge store) when the misapplied semantic involves a join.\n\n#### Ignored Instructions\n\n- **Pattern:** An author-written instruction is in place but Genie is not following it. Example: "always ask which processing date field to use" is ignored.\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Resolve conflicting instructions — an example SQL or other instruction may contradict the text instruction. Update or remove the conflicting item.\n  - Rephrase the text instruction more directly.\n  - Reduce text-instruction volume by moving logic into SQL expressions or column descriptions, so individual text instructions are not drowned out.\n  - Add an example SQL pair that demonstrates the desired behavior — examples often teach more reliably than text instructions.\n  - Hide irrelevant columns or simplify the schema with a view if the instruction is being ignored because the surrounding context is too noisy.\n\n#### Overconfidence\n\n- **Pattern:** Genie answers a question it shouldn\'t have. Example: a sales space is asked about on-time delivery rate; Genie misuses `date_closed` to approximate delivery times rather than declining.\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Add column descriptions and synonyms that make data boundaries explicit (what the column does and does not represent).\n  - Add a scope-limiting text instruction telling Genie which question categories to decline.\n  - For mission-critical answers, register a **trusted asset** (certified answer) so the verified response is always returned for that question.\n\n#### Time-based / timezone errors\n\n- **Pattern:** Wrong day boundaries, wrong timezone, or off-by-one date math.\n- **Recommended fixes:**\n  - Add an explicit timezone instruction naming the source timezone, target timezone, and the exact conversion function (e.g. `convert_timezone(...)`).\n  - Document the canonical "current day / week / month" boundary expression.\n\n#### Out-of-scope\n\n- **Pattern:** The user wants something Genie doesn\'t support — Python regression modeling, time-series forecasting, custom ML, ad-hoc visualization changes, etc.\n- **Recommended fixes:** Explicitly state the limitation. Suggest pre-aggregating in an upstream view, defining a metric view, or deferring to a notebook. Do not propose context changes that won\'t help.\n\n### Step 7 — Recommend concrete fixes\n\nFor each proposed fix, draft the **actual content** the author would add — not a label of "I\'ll add an instruction here." Examples:\n\n- "Add this SQL expression: `COUNT(DISTINCT message_id)` named `total_messages_sent`, with synonym \'total messages\'."\n- "Update column description on `events.opp_date` from \'(empty)\' to \'Date the opportunity was first opened. NOT the close date — use `cls_date` for close date.\' Add synonym `open date` to disambiguate from `cls_date`."\n- "Add this example SQL pair — Question: \'How many distinct users churned last month?\' SQL: `sql\\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) ...\\n`"\n- "Remove the conflicting text instruction \'count all events\' (it contradicts the new SQL expression)."\n- "Hide columns `internal_id`, `audit_user_id` from the `events` table — they\'re noise for end users."\n\nAfter listing the fixes, also propose a **benchmark question** that locks in the corrected answer (the original user prompt → the corrected expected SQL), so this regression can be detected in the future.\n\nGroup multiple changes by surface (instructions, snippets, column metadata, benchmarks) so the author can scan and accept the ones they want.\n\n## Output Format\n\nThe agent\'s natural-language commentary while running tools (steps 1-5) serves as the **thinking trace** — it should briefly describe what was retrieved at each step and why it\'s relevant. Tool-status pills handle the "what\'s running" part automatically; the trace adds interpretation.\n\nThe **final summary** at the end of the run should follow this structure:\n\n---\n\n**Diagnosis**\n\n- **What was wrong:** _[1 sentence describing the specific failure, e.g. "Genie returned 12,400 total messages but the correct value is 4,210 distinct messages."]_\n- **Root cause:** _[2-3 sentences. Reference specific items from the context applied — e.g., "Genie cited the text instruction \'Currency definition\' which only covers USD, but the user asked about EUR. There is no SQL expression for `total_messages_sent`, so Genie defaulted to `COUNT(*)`."]_\n\n> Internal note: keep your failure-mode classification (Incorrect SQL Logic / Inconsistency / Misapplied Semantics / Ignored Instructions / Overconfidence / Time-based / Out-of-scope) as a private mental label that drives which fixes you propose. Do **not** surface the label to the author in the final summary — the plain-language root cause already conveys what went wrong without the jargon.\n\n**Proposed fixes — review and accept the ones you want**\n\n1. **[Short title — what this changes]**\n\n   _[Concrete content the author would add or change, written out in full so they can read and approve it without guessing.]_\n\n2. **[Short title]**\n\n   _[Concrete content.]_\n\n3. ...\n\n**Lock in the correction with a benchmark**\n\n- **Question:** _[the original user prompt verbatim]_\n- **Expected SQL:**\n  ```sql\n  -- the corrected SQL\n  ```\n\n---\n\nAfter the summary, wait for the author to indicate which fixes to apply. When they do, call the corresponding edit tools (these all require approval anyway, so the author confirms in the tool dialog). Apply approved fixes in batches when natural — for example a single batched call covering all column-synonym changes for one table.\n\n## Optional Clarifications\n\n- **Ambiguous failure** — if step 3 determined the specific failure isn\'t clear, ask one targeted question before diagnosing. **Ground the question in the failure-mode taxonomy** — list the most likely modes given what you saw in step 2 (don\'t list all of them; pick 3-4 that fit), phrased in author-friendly language without mode names. The author\'s answer should pin down which mode you\'re in.\n\n  Template:\n\n  > "I read the message but want to make sure I\'m fixing the right thing. Which best describes what went wrong?\n  >\n  > - **Wrong number / wrong calculation** — the SQL ran but the math is off (e.g. `COUNT(*)` vs `COUNT(DISTINCT …)`).\n  > - **Wrong column or filter** — Genie picked a plausible-but-incorrect field (e.g. open date instead of close date).\n  > - **Conflicting definition** — the answer treats a business term differently than expected (e.g. churn defined two ways).\n  > - **Ignored an instruction** — Genie didn\'t follow guidance you\'ve already added to the space.\n  > - **Answered when it shouldn\'t have** — the question is outside what this space should answer.\n  > - **Something else** — describe it and I\'ll dig in."\n\n  Adapt this list to what step 2 showed. Examples of when to trim:\n  - If the message has `status: FAILED`, skip the menu and ask "What were you expecting Genie to return?" instead.\n  - If the SQL looks fine but the user mentioned a specific column, narrow to "Wrong column or filter" + "Something else".\n  - If two recent messages match the user\'s description, also ask which message: "I see two recent messages about churn — one yesterday (thumbs-down) and one today. Which should I diagnose?"\n\n- **Ambiguous resolution** — if you can\'t pick the right fix without input (e.g., two columns with similar names where either could be the canonical close date), quote both and ask which is canonical before drafting the change.\n- **Out-of-scope** — say so directly and stop. Do not propose context changes for capability gaps. Suggest the appropriate workaround (upstream view, metric view, notebook).\n\n## Important Notes\n\n- The failure-mode taxonomy is **guidance, not a fixed rule.** Propose the most appropriate fix for the specific issue, even if it differs from the recommendations for that mode.\n- In the final summary, never name internal tool/API identifiers (`addInstructionsToSpace`, `updateColumnSynonyms`, etc.) — describe what will change in the author\'s terms ("add a SQL expression…", "update the description on column X…", "remove this conflicting instruction"). Internal IDs (instruction IDs, snippet IDs) are likewise not user-facing — quote the human-readable title or content.\n- Always draft the **concrete content** of each proposed change so the author can review the wording before accepting. Don\'t ask "Confirm before I apply this?" — present the actual instruction text, the actual SQL expression, the actual column description, etc.\n- If `getMessageDetails` returns no `progress_report` attachments, smart-context info isn\'t available for that message; rely on the per-query-attachment cited fields and explicitly flag that smart-context retrieval data is missing.\n'}}]);
